name: gozleme bread
components:
  - name: dough
    ingredients:
      - item: full cream greek yogurt
        qty: 180
        unit: g
        note: 0.75 cup, add olive oil if low fat
      - item: plain flour
        qty: 165
        unit: g
        note: no leavening — gozleme is meant to stay flat
      - item: water
        optional: true
        note: by the tsp if the dough won't come together. plain flour is thirstier than SR
      - item: extra virgin olive oil
        qty: 1.5
        unit: tbsp
        note: for frying
      - item: salt
        qty: 2
        unit: g
        note: good pinch
      - item: lemon wedges
        note: for serving
    steps:
      - add flour, salt, and yogurt to a large bowl, mixing with a spoon or one hand to form a rough ball. add water a tsp at a time if it stays shaggy
      - transfer to a floured table and knead by hand for 3–5 mins until smooth. sprinkle more flour if too sticky or wet
      - rest covered for 20–30 mins. plain flour builds more gluten than SR, so it springs back when rolled — the long rest is what buys the thinness
  - name: filling
    ingredients:
      - item: spinach
        note: chopped
      - item: feta cheese
      - item: salt
      - item: pepper
    steps:
      - add chopped spinach to a bowl and scrunch with your hands to soften. then crumble in the feta cheese, add salt and pepper, and stir together
  - name: assembly and cook
    steps:
      - cut the dough into 2 equal pieces and roll them into balls
      - dust the table with flour and roll the dough into a thin circle (10–12 inches / 25–30 cm), flipping and sprinkling with flour regularly. roll as thin as possible without tearing
      - add half of the spinach mixture evenly to one half of the dough
      - fold the dough over the filling. press from the fold outward to push the air out before sealing the edges, otherwise you trap a pocket
      - prick the top through with a fork in a few places. the filling steams as it cooks and a sealed parcel has nowhere to vent — this is what balloons it, not the thickness
      - repeat with the 2nd dough
      - heat a stainless pan to 190 with 2 teaspoon olive oil. place a gozleme in the pan and cook for 3-5 minutes until golden
      - brush or spray the top with oil, flip it over using a spatula and gently press it to flatten it. keep it pressed as it cooks; prick and flatten any bubble that forms rather than letting it set inflated
      - when golden brown, remove from the pan and place on a board. repeat with the 2nd dough
      - slice into triangles, add a squeeze of lemon and enjoy!
  - name: variant — nagi's dough (recipetineats)
    notes: |
      https://www.recipetineats.com/gozleme/ — a completely different dough to the yogurt one
      above: flour, olive oil and water, no dairy at all. Yields 4 gozleme off 320g flour where
      the yogurt dough yields 2 off 165g. Nagi's is the more forgiving one to roll — she calls it
      elastic and non-tearing — and it fries crisper and more pastry-like, since the fat is worked
      through the dough rather than sitting in the pan. The yogurt dough is softer and more
      pliable eating. Same 20 min rest either way
    ingredients:
      - item: plain flour
        mass: 320
        qty: 2.5
        unit: cup
        note: plain/all purpose
      - item: extra virgin olive oil
        mass: 80
        qty: 80
        unit: ml
        note: 1/3 cup — this is in the dough, on top of the frying oil
      - item: water
        mass: 180
        qty: 180
        unit: ml
        note: 3/4 cup
      - item: cooking salt
        mass: 3
        qty: 0.5
        unit: tsp
        note: kosher
    steps:
      - combine flour, oil, salt and water in a stand mixer with the dough hook. mix 2.5 mins on medium-high (speed 7) until it forms a smooth pliable ball, not sticky
      - rest covered 20 mins
      - cut into 4 and roll each into a 35 x 20cm (14 x 8 inch) rectangle on a floured surface — rectangles, not the circles-folded-to-semicircles of the yogurt version
      - can be refrigerated up to 3 days after the rest
  - name: variant — nagi's spinach & feta filling
    notes: |
      Fills 4. Egg and mozzarella are the two things the simpler filling above doesn't have —
      the egg binds and enriches, the mozzarella is what actually makes it stretch and ooze.
      Hold the mozzarella out of the mixing bowl and add it at assembly
    ingredients:
      - item: baby spinach
        mass: 250
      - item: garlic
        qty: 2
        unit: clove
        note: minced
      - item: eggs
        qty: 2
        unit: ea
        note: large, ~50g each in shell
      - item: black pepper
        qty: 0.25
        unit: tsp
      - item: feta cheese
        mass: 250
        note: danish or greek
      - item: mozzarella
        qty: 2
        unit: cup
        note: shredded, tightly packed — added at assembly, not into the bowl
    steps:
      - put the spinach, garlic, egg and pepper in a bowl and scrunch with your hands to reduce the volume
      - crumble in the feta and stir through gently
      - add the mozzarella when assembling
  - name: variant — nagi's spiced lamb or beef filling
    notes: |
      Fills 4. Must be cooled before it goes near the dough — warm filling steams the pastry from
      the inside and you lose the crisp. Feta goes on at assembly, not into the pan
    ingredients:
      - item: olive oil
        qty: 1
        unit: tbsp
      - item: garlic
        qty: 2
        unit: clove
        note: minced
      - item: onion
        qty: 1
        unit: ea
        note: finely chopped
      - item: beef or lamb mince
        mass: 400
      - item: red capsicum
        qty: 1
        unit: ea
        note: small, diced
      - item: baby spinach
        mass: 90
      - item: cumin powder
        qty: 1.5
        unit: tsp
      - item: paprika
        qty: 1
        unit: tsp
      - item: cayenne
        qty: 0.25
        unit: tsp
      - item: cooking salt
        qty: 0.75
        unit: tsp
        note: kosher
      - item: black pepper
        qty: 0.25
        unit: tsp
      - item: water
        qty: 1
        unit: tbsp
      - item: feta cheese
        mass: 120
        note: danish or greek — crumbled in at assembly
    steps:
      - heat the oil in a skillet over high heat. cook the garlic and onion 1 min
      - add the capsicum, cook 2 mins
      - add the mince and cook until brown, breaking it apart
      - add the cumin, paprika, cayenne, salt, pepper and water, cook 2 mins
      - add the spinach and stir until wilted
      - cool before using. crumble the feta in at assembly
  - name: variant — nagi's assembly and cook
    steps:
      - spread the filling over half the pastry, pushing it right to the edges
      - fold the other half over. press the edges firmly, expelling the air pockets as you go. wet the edge with water if it won't seal
      - flour a pizza paddle and slide the gozleme onto it to move it
      - heat 1-1.5 tbsp olive oil in a large skillet over medium-high. slide the gozleme in and cook, pressing lightly, until deep golden and crispy — about 3 mins
      - flip and cook until crispy on the second side
      - cut into 6 or 8 pieces and serve immediately with lemon wedges
    notes: |
      Nagi doesn't prick the top the way the yogurt version above needs — the pressing-and-sealing
      does the air expulsion, and this dough has no leavening either. If it balloons anyway, prick
      and flatten it as per the main method; the cause is the same trapped steam
      Don't assemble ahead of time, the filling weeps and wets the pastry
      Cooked gozleme keeps 3 days in the fridge or freezes 3 months. Reheat by pan-frying for the
      crisp, or in the oven at 180°C for ~7 mins
      Works with gluten-free flour, but the dough is less stretchy — make 5 smaller ones
notes: |
  Self raising variant (the old default):
  - Swap the plain flour for 1 cup self raising flour
  - Rest 10 mins instead of 20–30 — softer flour, less springback
  - Puffed up badly even rolled thin. Two causes: the leavening, and steam with
    no vent. Adding 1 tsp baking powder to plain flour to fake SR flour makes it
    worse, so don't
  - The old note "AP flour seems not enough" was never resolved — unclear whether
    it meant not enough flour for the yogurt, or not enough lift. Moot now
  Untested with plain flour as of 2026-08-06 — the liquid may still need adjusting.

  Pushing it stretchier and less pastry-like (the 2026-09 result was too short):
  - Fat is the main culprit — fat coats gluten strands and stops them linking, which is
    what "short" means in shortcrust. Full cream greek yogurt is ~10% fat, so 180g of it
    is ~18g fat into 165g flour, around 11% baker's fat. Use plain non-greek low- or
    no-fat yogurt, or replace some of the yogurt with water. Ignore the "add olive oil if
    low fat" note above if chew is the goal, it pushes the wrong way
  - Yogurt's lactic acid degrades gluten — it's a tenderiser, and effective even over a
    30 min rest. Softness and tang come at the direct cost of chew; can't have both out
    of the one dough
  - Knead longer. 3-5 mins by hand is modest and chew needs the gluten actually developed,
    not just present. 8-10 mins, or knead 5 / rest 15 / knead 5, which makes the second
    knead far more effective
  - Bread flour over plain, for the obvious reason. Real but smaller than the three above
  - A touch more salt (1.5-2% of flour vs the current ~1.2%) strengthens gluten
  - Cook hotter and faster — 3-5 mins at 190°C is long for something this thin and dries
    the inside to crackery. Hotter pan, ~90 secs to 2 mins a side, colour fast then off.
    And stop pressing once you've got contact; sustained pressing squeezes moisture out
  - Stack them under a clean tea towel straight off the pan. Trapped steam softens the
    crust, and it's the cheapest thing on this list
  - For real chew rather than just softness, ~1% fresh yeast (or 1/4 tsp instant) and an
    hour — but that reopens the ballooning problem, so only with the pricking and pressing
  - Note that traditional gozleme dough is just flour, water and salt, no yogurt at all.
    If stretchy is the target the yogurt is what's in the way, and Nagi's variant is
    further from it, not closer — 80ml oil in 320g flour is ~25% fat, twice as short

  Two doughs, two characters: the yogurt dough (default above) is soft, tangy and pliable;
  Nagi's oil-and-water dough is crisper and more pastry-like, easier to roll thin, and scales
  to 4 rather than 2. Her fillings are richer than the plain spinach-and-feta above — egg and
  mozzarella in the veg one, and a whole spiced mince option — and either filling works with
  either dough.
